WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.371
<v Riley>Hey, welcome back. On today's episode — evacuation orders lifting at Gang Ranch, a gold discovery near the Cariboo mining project, and a deep dive that starts with sustainability skeptics needling Tom Szaky, then swings hard into an LA airport catering scandal to ask the real question: when the ESG label disappears, what's actually left holding working lands accountable out here? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:44.346 --> 00:01:06.506
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Tuesday, August 11th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Working Lands &amp; Industry.

00:01:07.201 --> 00:01:18.441
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today we're looking at who actually captures the value on the working landscape — ranching, forestry, mining, and the labor underneath all of it, wherever that labor happens to be sitting.

00:01:18.541 --> 00:01:43.701
<v Casey>Quick weather check — Horsefly Lake is sitting at 11 degrees this morning with clear skies, heading to a high of 22 and a low around 10, with a light drizzle expected tomorrow. 100 Mile House is at 11 and mainly clear, Williams Lake is at 13 with clear skies, Quesnel is at 12 and clear, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Alexis Creek is sitting at 12 and mainly clear. A calm-looking morning across the whole region.

00:01:44.448 --> 00:01:53.312
<v Riley>Good morning for anyone still watching the fire perimeter maps rather than the sky. Let's get into what's changed overnight and over the last few days.

00:01:54.712 --> 00:02:09.744
<v Riley>It's a fuller docket today — evacuation status shifting on two fronts, a mining discovery close to home, a billion-dollar settlement between two giants of the industry, and a science story with a surprisingly direct Cariboo angle before we even touch industry.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:27.294
<v Riley>ScienceDaily has a new piece on the fifteen-thousand-year relationship between humans and dogs — how selective breeding and companionship reshaped both species over that span, from early hunter-gatherer partnerships into the specialized working breeds we rely on today.

00:02:28.514 --> 00:02:33.746
<v Casey>Every rancher and outfitter in this region has strong opinions about which dog does which job.

00:02:35.381 --> 00:02:45.613
<v Casey>Closer to today's theme — the evacuation order for the Gang Ranch area has been lifted by the Cariboo Regional District. It's been downgraded to an evacuation alert as of yesterday.

00:02:47.072 --> 00:03:06.472
<v Riley>That's a working ranch operation, not a subdivision — cattle, hay crews, and a skeleton staff managing fire risk and livestock through this whole stretch. The alert still means residents need to be ready to leave again on short notice, but it's a meaningful step down, and it matters for anyone planning a fall roundup around it.

00:03:07.869 --> 00:03:22.157
<v Riley>A similar story played out further south. The Williams Lake Tribune reported evacuations were lifted in Spallumcheen as crews made progress on the Bradley Creek wildfire, which had already been running for over a week by the time the order came down.

00:03:23.344 --> 00:03:33.264
<v Casey>Different regional district, same pattern — containment progress translating directly into whether producers get back onto their land before the next cutting or shipping deadline.

00:03:34.898 --> 00:03:51.610
<v Casey>The Northern Miner reports Osisko Gold Group has made a discovery at its Proserpine target near the Cariboo gold project. Early drilling turned up assays as high as 95.93 grams per tonne gold over 4.6 metres, outlining a new mineralized system that could support future expansion.

00:03:52.739 --> 00:04:01.347
<v Riley>New discoveries adjacent to existing operations tend to extend mine life rather than create a wave of brand-new jobs — an incremental story, not a boomtown one.

00:04:02.969 --> 00:04:22.833
<v Riley>In the same sector on a much bigger scale, Barrick and Newmont have settled a Nevada dispute with a one-point-nine-billion-dollar deal, folding Barrick's Fourmile project and Newmont's Fiberline and Mike projects into Nevada Gold Mines. The Northern Miner frames it as clearing a hurdle for Barrick's planned North American IPO.

00:04:24.148 --> 00:04:36.036
<v Casey>A settlement that size between two of the largest gold miners on the planet is a useful reminder of the scale gap between global capital and a regional operation like Osisko's — different universe of money entirely.

00:04:37.457 --> 00:04:53.881
<v Casey>And on the mineral supply side, the Northern Miner reports the United States will block most exports of spent lithium-ion battery material, known as black mass, and tungsten scrap, for one year, citing national security concerns. Sellers will have to reserve that recycled feedstock domestically.

00:04:55.327 --> 00:05:07.383
<v Riley>A policy lever aimed squarely at critical mineral sovereignty — the next time someone argues battery recycling infrastructure doesn't matter geopolitically, here's a government actively restricting exports of it.

00:05:08.885 --> 00:05:37.221
<v Riley>Sticking with wildfire response — the 100 Mile Free Press reported the Cariboo Regional District and Thompson-Nicola Regional District had expanded evacuation alerts for the French Bar Creek and Pear Lake fires earlier this month. And CFJC Today Kamloops reported provincial officials told Cariboo MLA Lorne Doerkson that a formal state of emergency wasn't necessary at that point, even as Mexican firefighting crews were arriving at Abbotsford to help.

00:05:38.621 --> 00:05:44.965
<v Casey>International mutual aid showing up on our fire lines is becoming routine rather than remarkable at this point in the season.

00:05:46.485 --> 00:06:00.109
<v Casey>My Cariboo Now has an update on the Bald Range fire near Summerland and Penticton — a Summerland firefighter lost his own home to the fire but stayed on the front lines regardless. Evacuation areas there have been shrinking as crews gain ground.

00:06:01.465 --> 00:06:05.457
<v Riley>(thoughtful) Rough way to spend a shift, showing up to fight a fire that already took your house.

00:06:07.054 --> 00:06:18.758
<v Riley>BC Gov News has a story on training for transportation careers in Quesnel — a program helping people prepare for in-demand commercial trucking and logistics jobs tied to the resource sector.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:31.938
<v Casey>Globalnews.ca had a genuinely good-news piece out of the fire zone — two seniors who fled a B.C. wildfire were found safe because their dog was wearing an Apple AirTag, and strangers helped track the pair down using it.

00:06:33.106 --> 00:06:37.818
<v Riley>Cheap consumer hardware doing quiet lifesaving work — no press release needed for that one.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:54.950
<v Riley>Also today — the Williams Lake Tribune reports a new partnership aimed at strengthening health care across the Cariboo Chilcotin region, and the Perseid meteor shower has been lighting up Cariboo skies this week, if the smoke clears enough to actually see it.

00:06:56.073 --> 00:07:04.505
<v Casey>Scientific American confirmed world ocean temperatures hit a new record high for July — useful context for a lot of what we're watching burn this season.

00:07:06.026 --> 00:07:15.546
<v Casey>BC Gov News flagged that the province's first new medical school in nearly sixty years, the SFU Stephens Family School of Medicine, has started classes for its first cohort.

00:07:16.908 --> 00:07:38.004
<v Riley>One more that's adjacent to today's theme rather than inside it — The Walrus published a longer piece marking ten years since Colten Boushie's death, examining what that case revealed about rural RCMP contract policing and emergency response times in remote areas. Not a light read, but a relevant one for anyone thinking about rural governance.

00:07:39.808 --> 00:07:56.912
<v Casey>A couple of quick asides — Hackaday has a teardown of an Oxford Nanopore MinION DNA sequencer, the kind of portable lab gear that's shrunk dramatically over the past decade, and a piece on a homemade robotic screw and bolt sorter, a nice reminder of what hobbyist automation looks like at kitchen-table scale.

00:07:58.187 --> 00:08:07.819
<v Riley>And on the more sobering side, Al Jazeera reported China's Long March 7A rocket exploded shortly after lift-off from the Wenchang launch site — no word yet on cause.

00:08:08.465 --> 00:08:10.897
<v Casey>That's the roundup for today — into the spotlight.

00:08:12.297 --> 00:08:40.537
<v Riley>Today's spotlight is on the Williams Lake Food Policy Council. They work on local food security across the Cariboo — community gardens, food recovery programs that redirect surplus food instead of letting it go to waste, and farm-to-table connections that keep more of the food dollar in the region instead of leaking out to distant supply chains. If you've got a garden plot going unused or a farm with surplus at the end of the season, they're worth a conversation.

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:48.768
<v Casey>Food recovery work doesn't get headlines the way a mine discovery does, but it's exactly the kind of value-capture story we're chasing today.

00:08:49.091 --> 00:08:52.491
<v Riley>Which is a pretty clean bridge into where we're headed next.

00:08:53.891 --> 00:09:15.819
<v Riley>A Fast Company piece is making the rounds about Tom Szaky, the TerraCycle founder, arguing that sustainability skeptics keep declaring the movement dead, and keep being wrong. Szaky's whole career is built on the bet that plastic and waste, not just carbon, are the real fight — and that the fight didn't end when ESG became a punchline in some boardrooms.

00:09:16.363 --> 00:09:32.619
<v Casey>The data doesn't fully back the "keeps proving them wrong" framing. Generation Investment Management found the share of new funds marketed as sustainable dropped from fifty-nine percent in twenty-twenty-one to twenty-one percent by twenty-twenty-four. That's a collapse in one specific metric.

00:09:33.288 --> 00:09:58.288
<v Riley>A collapse in labeling, not necessarily in the underlying work. Seventeen Republican-run states passed anti-ESG bills last year restricting the use of state funds for that kind of investing, eight Democratic states passed pro-ESG bills in the same period — a political split down the middle, not a universal retreat. Plenty of companies are dropping the label while operations keep running the same way.

00:09:58.963 --> 00:10:17.979
<v Casey>Rebranding isn't nothing, but BlackRock and Vanguard pulling back from ESG commitments is a capital signal, not a marketing choice. Stanford's research shows young investors' support for ESG dropped sharply in that same window. When the people funding this walk away, the label mattering less usually means the substance became optional too.

00:10:18.517 --> 00:10:45.301
<v Riley>(pause:500) Bring that home to the Cariboo, though. A working ranch or a forestry operation here doesn't run on ESG fund flows in the first place — it runs on permits, supply contracts, and whoever's actually buying the product at the end of the season. The real question isn't whether Wall Street still likes the label. It's whether accountability mechanisms on the ground survive once the sustainability branding gets stripped away entirely.

00:10:45.844 --> 00:11:10.756
<v Casey>Which is exactly where the Flying Food Group story becomes relevant, even though it's an airport catering operation in Los Angeles, nowhere near the Cariboo. Workers filed a complaint with Cal/OSHA covering conditions at a facility serving LAX — maggots, cockroaches, mold, according to the union filing. It isn't the company's first citation, either. Cal/OSHA had already issued six citations in a prior incident, four of them classified as serious.

00:11:11.035 --> 00:11:14.323
<v Riley>That's a labor enforcement failure, not a technology gap.

00:11:14.935 --> 00:11:38.287
<v Casey>Right, and that's the whole point. Every time the industry's answer to a working-conditions problem is "we'll add sensors" or "we'll build a traceability dashboard," it dodges the actual lever — inspection capacity and whether workers have standing to force change. Six citations already on file and the company was still operating the same way when the second complaint landed. The problem was never a lack of information.

00:11:38.866 --> 00:11:59.890
<v Riley>Traceability systems don't replace inspectors, but they change who can see a problem developing before it takes six citations to register. A monitoring system that surfaces conditions in real time to a union rep, or to a health authority directly, is a genuinely different accountability structure than waiting for an annual audit a company knows is coming.

00:12:00.412 --> 00:12:18.500
<v Casey>That only works if someone with actual power is watching the dashboard and willing to act on what they see. Cal/OSHA presumably had paperwork on Flying Food Group after the first round of citations too. The gap wasn't information — it was consequence, a fine small enough that repeating the violation was still cheaper than fixing it.

00:12:19.188 --> 00:12:46.948
<v Riley>(pause:500) Bring it back to forestry and ranching here, where the equivalent isn't a catering warehouse but a logging contractor, a meat-processing plant, or a seasonal orchard crew. WorkSafeBC inspection capacity in rural BC has been stretched thin for years — a documented staffing shortfall, not a missing gadget. A sensor or a camera doesn't fix a shortage of inspectors who can physically show up to a site three hours from the nearest office.

00:12:47.542 --> 00:13:13.846
<v Casey>And that's the real parallel to the ESG retreat story, not the surface-level "sustainability is dying" argument. When these commitments were fashionable, companies over-invested in the labels — certifications, carbon pledges, glossy community benefit language in the annual report. Now that the label costs political capital instead of buying it, a lot of that gets quietly dropped, and what's left standing is whatever was legally binding in the first place, nothing more.

00:13:14.445 --> 00:13:35.605
<v Riley>Impact Benefit Agreements tied to permits and Aboriginal title survive ownership changes and market swings precisely because they're not voluntary branding — they're contractual obligations that outlast whoever signed them. The ESG retreat almost proves the inverse case: voluntary commitments evaporate exactly when they become inconvenient.

00:13:36.196 --> 00:13:55.764
<v Casey>So Szaky "proving skeptics wrong" is proving something narrower than the headline suggests. His own business model — recycling and packaging waste — survives the culling because there's a paying customer for that specific outcome regardless of ESG fashion in any given year. That's not the same as sustainability broadly surviving as a movement.

00:13:56.426 --> 00:14:21.314
<v Riley>(chuckles) It's the least romantic possible answer, but it's the right one — TerraCycle's model works because someone, a brand or a retailer, pays directly for the waste stream to disappear. That's a functioning market, not a moral commitment dressed up in language. The lesson for working lands here isn't "adopt more sustainability branding." It's "find the actual paying customer for the specific outcome you want to preserve."

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:44.380
<v Casey>Which is where the Williams Lake Food Policy Council's food recovery work is honestly a better model than anything in the Fast Company piece. Redirecting surplus food to people who need it isn't waiting on ESG fund flows or a corporate pledge that could get cut next fiscal year — it's a direct, local transaction that doesn't depend on a label staying fashionable in New York or anywhere else.

00:14:44.913 --> 00:15:06.441
<v Riley>That's the real durability test. Does the arrangement keep functioning when nobody outside the region is paying attention to it anymore? A food recovery program built on standing local relationships passes that test far better than a carbon offset program tied to a corporate sustainability report a new CFO can quietly kill in one quarterly earnings call.

00:15:07.066 --> 00:15:24.826
<v Casey>Same test applies to forestry contracts and ranching leases here. Nation-owned forestry operations in this region have moved toward internalizing fire mitigation costs into baseline operating contracts, as reported locally, rather than treating them as a discretionary environmental add-on that can get trimmed in a lean year.

00:15:25.504 --> 00:15:39.248
<v Riley>Because it isn't branded as sustainability at all in the way Wall Street would recognize the word — it's built into how the operation is structured to keep functioning through a bad fire season without a special fund or a press release.

00:15:39.795 --> 00:16:06.899
<v Casey>That's a sharper distinction than the usual "label versus substance" framing gets credit for. It isn't about whether a company still uses the word "sustainable" in its annual report this year or drops it next year under political pressure. It's about whether the commitment is written into a contract, a permit condition, or a Nation's actual ownership stake — something with legal teeth behind it — or whether it was always a voluntary gesture that gets dropped the moment capital gets more expensive.

00:16:07.424 --> 00:16:27.352
<v Riley>For a listener running a guest ranch, a guiding outfit, or a small sawmill here in the Cariboo, the practical question is: which of your actual commitments — to your guides, to the land base, to seasonal staff — are written into something binding, versus which ones are just current management's preference that could evaporate under new ownership.

00:16:27.959 --> 00:16:44.095
<v Casey>And which of those would survive if you sold the business tomorrow to someone from outside the region who's never met a single guide on staff. If the honest answer is none of them, the sustainability language was decoration the whole time, not a description of how the business actually runs.

00:16:44.632 --> 00:17:02.096
<v Riley>A useful audit for any working-lands operation around here — mining, forestry, ranching, guiding, food processing, doesn't matter which sector. Look hard at what's contractually binding versus what's just current management's stated preference, and be honest about which pile is bigger.

00:17:02.766 --> 00:17:22.070
<v Casey>The Flying Food Group case is the warning label on what happens when almost nothing but preference is holding the line — six serious citations and the facility was still operating the same way when workers filed again. Enforcement capacity and legal standing close that gap, not better branding, and there's no reason to think it'll be different here.

00:17:22.587 --> 00:17:39.515
<v Riley>We're not fully aligned on how much the technology piece matters in closing that gap. A monitoring tool that gets real inspection data in front of the right person faster still changes outcomes at the margins, even if it's clearly not sufficient on its own to fix an enforcement shortfall.

00:17:40.104 --> 00:17:59.224
<v Casey>And that assumes the "right person" receiving that data has the authority and the will to act on it — which the Cal/OSHA repeat-citation record doesn't really support as a safe assumption. The open question isn't whether better information exists somewhere in a dashboard. It's who's structurally accountable to actually use it when it shows a problem.

00:17:59.905 --> 00:18:27.457
<v Riley>(warmly) That's a good place to land this one — not resolved, but sharpened into something more specific than where we started. If you know of a Cariboo operation where a contract or a formal agreement, rather than a sustainability label, is what's actually holding a commitment in place through a hard season, send it to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a — we'd genuinely like specific examples from listeners who've seen this play out on the ground.

00:18:28.032 --> 00:18:34.800
<v Casey>I'll still want proof it holds up under new ownership before I call it durable, but that's the right thing to go looking for.

00:18:35.142 --> 00:18:38.862
<v Riley>Thanks for spending part of your Tuesday with us out here in the Cariboo.

00:18:39.391 --> 00:18:47.591
<v Casey>(wry) And if the smoke clears out tonight, it's worth stepping outside for the Perseids before this fire season takes that view away too — see you next time.